\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple undirected graph with $n=|V|\ge1$ and $m=|E|$. Its adjacency matrix is $A$, its diagonal degree matrix is $D$, and its combinatorial Laplacian is $L=D-A$. Order the real eigenvalues as $\lambda_1\ge\cdots\ge\lambda_n=0$, counting multiplicity.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every such graph $G$ and every integer $k$ with $1\le k\le n$, is
\[
 \sum_{i=1}^k\lambda_i\le m+\binom{k+1}{2}?
\]
\href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.03447v1}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Is the sum of any k largest graph Laplacian eigenvalues at most the number of edges plus $k(k+1)/2$?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 1388 (GRAPH-001). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Joshua N. Cooper, \emph{Constraints on Brouwer's Laplacian Spectrum Conjecture} (2020), arXiv:2003.03447v1, Section 1, equation (1), pp. 1--2. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.03447v1}.
\end{description}
